>> The Florida Public Records Law has been construed to cover electronic mail
>> messages. The University may maintain a copy of your e-mail messages on
>> magnetic tape for up to one year in compliance with law. 

Note the use of the words "may maintain."  Nothing says that the University
*must* maintain these archives.

>This looks to me not like they *could* make your email public if for some
>reason it was necessary, but that they're actively archiving all posts.
>All sounds very dubious to me.

Indeed.

Some years ago, this university made a similar finding.  When we received
notice that email could fall under Open Meetings law (the Kentucky version
of the "sunshine" law), I (with my supervisor's concurrence) made the sim-
ple decision; we simply stopped archiving electronic mail.  Email is the
sole item not included in our backup schemes.  Our users know about this
policy, and we haven't received any complaints.  If a user does want a
piece of email archived, they merely save it as a file in their diskspace;
it is then a "user file," which is automatically included in backups.

It has not been tested, but I believe that "user files" enjoy a level of
protection above that of electronic mailboxes.  One can make the argument
that mailboxes are "system space," but user files are maintained in pri-
vate space.  I do not believe that a request under the Open Meetings law
would empower me (as an admin) to go rooting through user files; in any
case, I fervently hope that it does not.

>Btw the university is CFRVM.BITNET - don't know what that is in real life.

According to my copy of BITEARN NODES (the master list of BITNET/EARN
sites), CFRVM is the "Florida Central Regional Data Center."  This
sounds like some sort of cooperative venture; it may involve several
universities.

--Wes

