<A NAME="2bmrt2$j3f@eff.org">  Re: Policy from the Stevens Institute of Technology login</a><pre>
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject:  Re: Policy from the Stevens Institute of Technology login
Date: 8 Nov 1993 20:31:14 -0500
Message-ID: <2bmrt2$j3f@eff.org>
A critique.

Summary: Due process is poor with punishment proceeding "conviction"
by special computer judicial committee. Privacy protection is vague
without details of how keystroke monitoring of users is authorized.
Free expression protection is poor with some programs and (apparently)
pseudonymous expression banned.

>	Unauthorized use is not allowed; keystroke monitoring by 
>	authorized personnel may be in effect when system security or 
>	integrity is in question.  By using this system, you expressly 
>	consent to such monitoring and any resultant legal actions.

This is actually better than the CERT original. At least the criteria
for monitoring authorized users is "when system security or integrity
is in question" rather than when a authorized user is suspected of
exceeding his or her authority. Also it says "keystroke monitoring"
rather than just "monitoring".

What it doesn't do is detail how Institute personnel get authorization
to monitor the keystrokes of authorized users. Is every Institute
employee preauthorized to start monitoring on his or her own? Or does
authorization come from some higher official on a case-by-case basis?
The _Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students_ recommends:

  Except under extreme emergency circumstances, premises occupied by
  students and the personal possessions of students should not be
  searched unless appropriate authorization has been obtained. For
  premises such as residence halls controlled by the institution, an
  appropriate and responsible authority should be designated to whom
  application should be made before a search is conducted. The
  application should specify the reasons for he search and the objects
  or information sought. The student should be present, if possible,
  during the search. For premises not controlled by the institution,
  the ordinary requirements for lawful search should be followed.

If you believe that academic freedom principles should apply to
computers, this could easily be extended to computer monitoring.

>        ANTI-WATCHDOG programs are prohibited on the VAXcluster.
[...]
>Sending electronic mail or messages to other users at Stevens (or elsewhere)
>UNDER A PHONEY USERNAME is not permitted.  Having mail-sending programs which 
>falsify the sender's identity is also not allowed since this type of program 
>is only "useful" for sending phoney mail.  
[...]

Mere possession of programs should not be banned. Programs are
expressive material and should be protected by the Institute's
commitment to free expression. At most the Institute should require
that banned-from-running programs be disabled so they can't be
executed accidently.

As to prohibiting "phoney" usernames. "Phoney" was an interesting word
choice. I believe it comes from the early days of the telephone when
people wuold make fraudulant phone calls pretending to be someone
else. If the Institute only means to prohibit fraudulent
impersonation, then I wish them all the best. If, on the other hand,
they also mean to prohibit all pseudonymous and anonymous expression
than I think they go to far. As the Supreme Court said in _Tally v.
California_: "{a}nonymously pamphlets, leaflets, brochures and even
books have played an important role in the progress of mankind.
Persecuted groups and sects from time to time throughout history have
been able to criticize oppressive practices and laws either
anonymously or not at all."

>	Sending "chain" letters is not permitted on Stevens campus
>	network or externally out across Internet, BITNET, etc.
>	This is considered a serious offense by the guidelines of
>	the Network Review Board.  Violators are subject to loss
>	of access to the academic VAXcluster pending deliberation/decision
>	by the Network Review Board (student policy-enforcing group.)
[...]

This reminds me a line from Lewis Carroll's _Alice in Wonderland_:

   "No, no," said the Queen: "The sentence first -- the verdict
    afterwards."

In contrast, the Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students
says:

   Pending action on the charges, the status of a student should not
   be altered, or his right to be present on the campus and to attend
   classes suspended, except for reasons relating to his physical or
   emotional safety and well being, or for reasons relating to the
   safety and well-being of students, faculty, or university property.

Also, how does the Network Review Board fit in the the Institute's
larger due process procedures? What training does the Board get?
Respecting due process is a difficult and important task that should
only be given to committees with extensive training. Most academic
institutions would do better to treat computer infractions like other
school infractions, rather than trying to set up a computer center
specific judicial system.

- Carl

